ologyNonfictionProgrammingBusinessEngineeringComputer Science\n\n...more\n\n384 pages, Kindle Edition\n\nPublished October 21, 2025\n\n### About the author\n\n#### Gene Kim\n\n25 books 1,041 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nGene Kim is a multiple award-winning CTO, Tripwire founder, Visible Ops co-author, IT Ops/Security Researcher, Theory of Constraints Jonah, a certified IS auditor and a rabid UX fan.\nHe is passionate about IT operations, security and compliance, and how IT organizations successfully transform from \"good to great.\"\n\n## Ratings & Reviews\n\nWhat do you think?\n\nWrite a Review\n\n* * *\n\n### Friends & Following\n\nCreate a free account to discover what your friends think of this book!\n\n### Community Reviews\n\n3.70\n\n458 ratings 62 reviews\n\n5 stars\n\n114 (24%)\n\n4 stars\n\n154 (33%)\n\n3 stars\n\n143 (31%)\n\n2 stars\n\n33 (7%)\n\n1 star\n\n14 (3%)\n\nSearch review text\n\n[Input: Search review text]\n\nFilters\n\nDisplaying 1 - 30 of 61 reviews\n\nŁukasz\n\n53 reviews 8 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nNovember 1, 2025\n\nWhen something seems easy, usually one of three things is happening: you’re a very smart individual, the Dunning-Kruger effect is at play, or the thing is actually easy. In my personal experience, the first option can be quickly ruled out, so I usually bet on the second one. That’s why I was hoping for some deep and unexpected insights from this book. However, there don’t seem to be many, which suggests that AI-assisted software development might just not be that hard.\nIf you’ve already tried coding with an agent, you’ve probably come to most of the same conclusions as this book. The agent messed up your project? Ouch, you need to commit more often and proceed step by step. Thinking about parallelization? The code needs clear boundaries and modular design. Want to maintain quality? You have to check everything carefully. The application doesn’t do what you had in mind? Maybe start with tests. And so on. It’s all pretty easily figure-out-able.\nThe last parts of the book are more interesting, as they explore scaling the process, but the advice remains superficial, nothing you couldn’t come up with yourself.\nSo the best advice is simply to start coding with an agent. If you actually struggle, then this book might be worth reaching for, but most of the time it probably won’t be necessary.\n\nHaving a leader board for tokens burned is dumb, you might as well say leaderboard for money burned.\nSomeone new to using agents to help with code might get some tips from the book, but as someone who has used Claude Code and others extensively I got zero value from it.\n\n11 likes\n\n2 comments\n\nLike\n\n\nBharath\n\n1,034 reviews 669 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nApril 13, 2026\n\nOther than the AI excitement around human like language comprehension & writing, image generation, another area which has advanced rapidly is AI-assisted Vibe Coding. Apparently, Dr Andrej Karpathy, AI Pioneer coined the term. The authors – Steve and Gene have different backgrounds & strengths, but both now enjoy being hands-on with Vibe Coding. They mention that many programmers are now coming back from retirement to code.\nLarge part of the book is conceptual, and this should not be considered a reference guide on Vibe Coding. Since becoming popular 2 years back, the advance is rapid and has evolved from chat-based programming to agentic development. There were 2 rounds of run-time errors which the AI was able to resolve quickly. I have to agree with the authors that Vibe Coding is an enjoyable experience. The more detailed examples are few though.\nA good introductory book.\n\n10 likes\n\n4 comments\n\nLike\n\nComment\n\nErika RS\n\n921 reviews 276 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nDecember 30, 2025\n\nFirst off, the elephant: Yes, it's called vibe coding and that feels a little cringe. No, it's not about never-look-at-the-code, YOLO vibe coding. The book itself is full of high quality content. It's unfortunate the the relatively speed of publishing cycles for books makes choosing titles for books in this space hard.\nSecond, disclosure: I know Steve and worked with him the past several years. He and I discussed many of the ideas in this book as they were being developed.\nThird, this book has a really cool cover. I love the rainbow matrix vibe.\nThis book is most valuable to folks who have experimented with AI-assisted coding, kind of get it, but want to be more effective. And when paired with Steve Yegge’s Medium articles on Beads, the vision becomes tangible: a blueprint for orchestrating AI agents to build production-grade software with precision, speed and creativity.\nThe question isn’t whether AI will change software development—it’s whether you’re ready to step into the future and lead the change.\nI strongly recommend Vibe Coding for senior engineers, tech leads, architects and engineering managers who are looking to not just “experiment with AI coding assistants”, but to strategically transform how their teams build software. Developers who are curious about stepping into this next frontier should read it too—it will challenge your assumptions about what coding is.\nThis book doesn’t just predict the future, it attempts to architect it. It says: the writing is on the wall–AI, agents, conversational dev are here and you either adopt the orchestration mindset now or risk being left playing catch-up. If you approach it with both optimism and discipline (the book emphasises hardening and governance), you can ride the wave rather than be swamped by it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVibe Coding: Building Production-Grade Software With GenAI, Chat, Agents, and Beyond by Gene Kim | Goodreads (https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/228438060-vibe-coding)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: today; If you've been working with AI coding tools for a while, the first two-thirds of Vibe Coding will feel like familiar ground.Kim and Yegge cover solid, practical advice — but for practitioners already deploying AI agents in their daily workflow or even at the organizational level, much of it reads as confirmation rather than revelation. ... If you’re already using a tool like Claude Code or GitHub Copilot for coding with AI, this will give some good tips and tricks from an engineering manager who just got back to coding after a long time because he found agentic AI.\nPaul Duplys\n\n25 reviews\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nApril 29, 2026\n\nIf you've been working with AI coding tools for a while, the first two-thirds of Vibe Coding will feel like familiar ground. Kim and Yegge cover solid, practical advice — but for practitioners already deploying AI agents in their daily workflow or even at the organizational level, much of it reads as confirmation rather than revelation. Somewhere around the halfway mark, I'll admit, my enthusiasm started to dip.\nThen the final third hits — and the book earns its place on the shelf.\nThat last section is a genuine treasure chest. Kim and Yegge stop recapping the current state of the art and start pointing at what comes next: the deeper shifts in how development teams operate, how AI agency scales beyond individual productivity, and what \"the next level\" actually looks like for professionals who think they've already figured this out.\nIf you're new to AI-assisted development, read it cover to cover. If you're already a power user — push through the familiar first half. The payoff is real, and it's in the back.\n\nJack\n\n109 reviews 6 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nSeptember 23, 2026\n\nI’m going to give this 3.5 stars—3 if you are a battle-scarred senior software developer and 4 if you are informally trained. Depends on what you know coming in.\nFor a book at the early part of the adoption curve this isn’t bad. The first third is mostly “holy cow look at all the things we can do.” Not particularly useful because I feel like we are all collectively doing the same thing. What’s good is these authors have been around.\nThe last 3rd of the book is looking ahead at best practices and how to change your organization—that is the best part. I expect because this book is ahead of the curve that better ideas will evolve over time, but you know what, it does have good ideas.\n\nGoke Pelemo\n\n37 reviews 1 follower\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nDecember 15, 2025\n\nSuper helpful!\nIf you’re already using a tool like Claude Code or GitHub Copilot for coding with AI, this will give some good tips and tricks from an engineering manager who just got back to coding after a long time because he found agentic AI. Revitalizing old projects, taking on new challenges rapidly, and being very productive.\nIf you’re wondering how coding with AI can fit into the work you do, this will provide a starter kit with some inspiration to boot.\nIt was a fun read.\n\nNish\n\n17 reviews 21 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nJanuary 18, 2026\n\nLots of respect for Steve and love his blogging. On an interview about this book, he said something like \"we had too much content for a blog post (even for me, ha ha!) so we had to write a book instead\".\nAbout 1/3 through the book now and have found the prose to be repetitive and shallow (despite the authors bragging about vibe coding a system to check their prose for repetitive ideas...). I don't doubt the skill of the authors, but so far, it has not been super insightful if you're an engineer who has been using these tools already.\n\nJustin Salér\n\n93 reviews\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nFebruary 14, 2026\n\nIt's a bold challenge to write a book about something you know will change every week from it's release. If you take away technology which has been outdated in the last few months, there are some good viewpoints and predictions that may not be predictions in another year. But while the authors have profound and contemplated perspectives, a lot of the scaffolding looks like my LinkedIn feed. And I am left with a feeling that I should've asked ChatGPT to give me an updated version of the book considering the state-of-the-art tools of agentic engineering.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVibe Coding: Building Production-Grade Software With GenAI, Chat, Agents, and Beyond by Gene Kim | Goodreads (https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/228438060-vibe-coding)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last month; Last time I was writing computer code regularly more than 20 years ago, planning to also try vibe coding in the near future, probably for some productivity and home automation stuff. ... 📘 Vibe Coding: Building Production-Grade Software With GenAI, Chat, Agents, and Beyond by Gene Kim (The Phoenix Project) and Steve Yegge is the definitive manifesto for the next era of development.\nFerhat Elmas\n\n928 reviews 46 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nDecember 7, 2025\n\nBuilt on big names and good stories, it might even be seen entertaining but far too light. It leans on metaphors and slogans instead of tackling the hard questions: how to measure quality, how to make the process repeatable, where AI actually breaks things, what it means for security, and how it reshapes engineering culture. It never offers anything you couldn’t figure out yourself just by using the tools. In the end, the book talks about vibe coding more than it gives you a real way to do it with rigor.\n\nTõnu Vahtra\n\n629 reviews 99 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nJanuary 16, 2026\n\nLast time I was writing computer code regularly more than 20 years ago, planning to also try vibe coding in the near future, probably for some productivity and home automation stuff. I believe that the most people/organizations are not really ready to realize the benefits from the AI empowerment in programming and it should mostly be viewed as an advanced automation solution, that requires a lot of critical thinking and sensemaking on a continuous basis. Tatu Saloranta\n\n40 reviews 1 follower\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nNovember 12, 2025\n\nHard to rate: liked a lot, disliked some (too fluffy, abstract). So 3 or 4 stars?\n\nRahul Mahindru\n\n88 reviews 4 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nNovember 1, 2025\n\nToo basic\n\nZachary Lysobey\n\n31 reviews\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nJanuary 16, 2026\n\nI was torn between 3/5 and 4/5 on the rating here.\nThere is some great content, but I do feel like to book was quite a bit longer than it needed to be, and by the end I was really dragging.\nFor people who are good at picking-and-choosing chapters, I think this could be great. But I have a cover-to-cover compulsion.\nI also read Addy Osmani's \"Beyond Vibe Coding\" book. The scope of things there was quite different, but this book felt more refined and better put together. I think the content in this book is more likely to stay relevant longer than \"Beyond Vibe Coding\", so that book getting a bit rushed out the door was probably not the worst choice however.\n6. AI as a Teammate: Treat AI as a teammate rather than just a tool, requiring conscientious oversight, reviewing, validating, and testing AI-generated code more than usual.\n7. Managing Complexity: Breaking down complex tasks into smaller, manageable parts that AI can handle effectively is crucial. This involves using \"tracer bullets\" to ensure a complete path through the system works.\n8. Parallel Work and Workflow Automation: Leveraging AI for parallel work and investing in workflow automation can significantly enhance productivity. This includes using version control effectively and documenting standards thoroughly.\n9. Avoiding Context Saturation: Managing AI's context effectively is important to prevent performance degradation due to context saturation. This involves using focused or comprehensive context management strategies as needed.\n10. Continuous Learning and Adaptation: Embracing continuous learning and adapting to fast-changing environments is vital for thriving in a vibe coding world.\n\n\nMaya Senen\n\n466 reviews 23 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nFebruary 1, 2026\n\nIf you are new to vibe coding, or maybe new to coding in general, this is probably a helpful stepping off point. If you've been at it at least a while, maybe slinging between two to three agents, there are still some useful tips in here. I walked away with a handful of practical additions to my approach. If you are a seasoned individual, who has already settled on your workflow and solved some of the classic problems at this point (token context, hallucinations, inappropriate task definition), this is definitely too many words on the subject. Carry on. If you are on the fence or have a few reservations, this book may help you iron out your position or convince you to give vibe coding a shot. Nevertheless, the barrier to entry still seems to be tokens - if you have a limited investment, it seriously hamstrings your development cycles. While you can work on the basics and principles outlined here, you may not be having as much fun.\n\nYevhenii Kanivets\n\n4 reviews\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nAugust 20, 2026\n\nWhile reading this book about vibe coding, I couldn't shake off the feeling it was also vibe-written. I had the same urge to fast-forward as while reading the answers generated by LLMs 🥱\nThere were many interesting ideas and learnings, but certainly not enough for a 300+ page book. It would be more appropriate as 100 page book, or simply a dozen of Medium articles.\nYou can get a better understanding of the vibe coding if you spend the same amount of time with Claude Code directly, no book needed 😆\n\nDan Lewis\n\n125 reviews 1 follower\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nOctober 30, 2025\n\nI've been following Steve Yegge for many years. He formed my junior opinions on so many topics in software. It's not an exaggeration to credit him with inspiring me to go to Amazon, which changed my life forever.\nThe promise of AI is multifaceted for the lay person in 2025. We can use it to fake coherent videos and there are huge controversies and lawsuits around using copyrighted training data. Jakub Anderwald\n\n13 reviews 1 follower\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nDecember 31, 2025\n\nThis is a good entry-level book for someone who has no clue about vibe coding. It gives a few starting points, watchouts and specifics of working with AI agents.\nUnfortunately, it doesn't provide much more than that. Neither does it go deeper into explaining how AI agents / models work, not does it give specific solutions to the problems it highlights when using vibe coding on a professional scale.\nIt feels more like a conversation you would have with a few people you've met at a vibe coding conference where they share their stories and pieces of knowledge they have. Actually, a lot of the book contents is based on personal experiences of the authors, rather than large-scale studies or papers published by the companies in the field.\n\nAugustinas\n\n59 reviews\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nJanuary 20, 2026\n\nA bit mixed bag, book reads like written by LLM (authors say it is), meaning authors optimize for writer, not reader. Lots of duplication, same few examples are dragged through every chapter.\nAuthors bring a lot of good points, while I don't agree with some, overall I think book is great for any AI skeptic tech leader to read and consider more perspectives.\n\nAlin Tuhut\n\n1 review 3 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nNovember 21, 2025\n\nI am sorry but this book was most likely \"vibe written\".\n\nJustin\n\n10 reviews\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nApril 1, 2026\n\nI read this book to try and get useful insights on how to use AI beyond vibecoding. I did not find that in this book. Now excuse me while I go off on the biggest rant for a book I may ever write.\nI am in software, and I wanted to like this book so much. I forced myself to get through the whole book to give it a fair shot (I even read the “just checking if you’re reading care